## ABSTRACT
A novelly-defined adsorption membrane for rapid removal of fluoride from drinking water was prepared. Both zirconium metal-organic frameworks ( $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs) adsorbent and membrane with large specific surface area of $740.28~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ were used for fluoride removal for the first time. For adsorption technique, fluoride adsorption on $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs was studied on a batch mode. The adsorption data could be well described by Langmuir isotherm model while the adsorption kinetic followed pseudo-second-order model. The maximum of adsorption capacity was $102.40~\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $\mathrm{pH}~7.0$ when the initial fluoride concentration was $200~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The FT-IR and XPS analyses of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs revealed that both surface hydroxyl groups and $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ active sites played important roles in fluoride adsorption process. The as-prepared $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs adsorbent was suitable for practical treatment of drinking water and regeneration by sodium hydroxide solution $3\%$ wt). For membrane experiments, Zr-MOFs membrane supported on Alumina substrate could remove fluoride efficiently through dynamic filtration. The fluoride removal capability of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane depended on flow rate and initial concentration of fluoride. The fluoride removal abilities of $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs membrane with $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness could reach 5510, 5173, and $4664~\mathrm{L/m}^{2}$ when fluoride concentrations were 5, 8 and $10\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively. This study indicated that Zr-MOFs membrane could be developed into a very viable technology for highly effective removal of fluoride from drinking water.
Keywords: Zr-MOFs; Membrane; Fluoride; Adsorption; Mechanism
## 1. Introduction
Fluoride (F) is an essential element for both human and animals while it is also one of the World Health Organization (WHO) classified contaminants in ground water, maximum acceptable concentration limiting in $1.5~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ [1]. In a narrow concentration ranged of $0.5{-}1.5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , fluoride contained water would be good for the health of teeth and bones [2-4]. However, excess ingested of fluoride through food and drinks can cause a series of diseases such as dental/skeletal fluorosis, fetal cerebral function, and neurotransmitters [5-7]. Taking the serious health effects into account, many technologies have been developed and evaluated for the removal of excess amounts of fluoride from drinking water: precipitation [8], electro-coagulation [9-11], membrane techniques [12, 13] and adsorption [2, 7, 8, 14-17]. Despite the availability of above mentioned processes for fluoride removal, adsorption and membrane separation still remain to be two of the best methods.
Commonly in the removal process of fluoride, the two methods of adsorption and membrane separation were used separately. Adsorption can be defined as the immobilization of target molecules by adsorbents through biological recognition, electrostatic interactions, chemical reactions and hydrophobic interactions. Developing high-capacity and selective adsorbents was essential to facilitate fluoride removal in order to meet the requirement for the drinking water. So far, different adsorbents have been exploited for fluoride removal, such as alumina [18] and alumina based adsorbents [19, 20], mixed oxides [21-23], activated carbon [24], clay like materials [25, 26], bio-polymer based composites [27] and ion-exchange resins [28]. This method can remove soluble and insoluble organic pollutants easily and effectively without generation of hazardous by-products [29]. However, the adsorbents were sometimes difficult to be separated from water in practical application [8, 30]. Membrane separation was defined as the rejections of target molecules by size exclusion [31]. In the traditional membrane separation processes, size exclusion was difficult to achieve, but the membrane can be easily separated from water [32, 33]. Thus, the combination of adsorption and membrane separation can remove fluoride more effectively and will not cause secondary pollution [34]. It was highly important to find materials that can be used as both adsorbent and membrane.
Recently, a new class of fluoride adsorbent, named metal organic frameworks (MOFs), attracts great attention because of their tunable crystalline organic-inorganic hybrid networks [35-37]. MOFs can exhibit superior physical and chemical characteristics and have been applied in various adsorption-related areas, including hydrogen storage [38-40], $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption [41, 42] and removal of toxic gases [43, 44]. Due to the promising potential in the areas mentioned above, researchers started to investigated the capability of MOFs to remove compounds in aqueous environment. A number of researches have been conducted including removal of metal ions [45], toxic dyes [46, 47], herbicides [48], oil droplets [49], humic acid [50]. These studies unveiled that several types of MOFs can remain intact in aqueous solutions and show exceptional capacities to remove pollutants from water compared to traditional adsorbents. Lately, MOFs has also been evaluated for defluoridation in water [51-53]. It was found that MOFs exhibited high adsorption capacity for fluoride compared to other adsorbents. However, there were very few researches about MOFs membrane for fluoride removal.
In the present study, a novelly-defined adsorption membrane for rapid removal of fluoride from aqueous solution was prepared for the first time. Both adsorption and membrane techniques were used for fluoride removal. Zr-MOFs adsorbent and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane were prepared and used for fluoride removal, respectively. For adsorption experiments, fluoride adsorption isotherms and kinetics of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs adsorbent were investigated. The effects of pH and co-existing anions were examined. The mechanism of fluoride removal was studied by FT-IR and XPS analyses. The as-prepared adsorbent was also used for treatment of underground water with high fluoride concentration in Xingwang Village, Inner Mongolia of China. For membrane experiments, the effects of temperature, flow rate and initial concentration of fluoride on fluoride removal were studied, the amount of fluoride contaminated water that $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane can deal with was also tested.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of Zr-MOFs membrane and Zr-MOFs adsorbent
The $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane was fabricated on alumina substrate (diameter:3.0 cm, thickness: 0.1 cm) by an in-situ solvothermal synthesis method in Fig. S1 (see in Supporting Information). 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid was used as a surface modifier of the substrate during the synthesis via constructing coordination bonds between the carboxylate oxgens and aluminum atoms from substrate. The optimized recipe is: $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ ${>}99.5\%$ , Sigma Aldrich), 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC, $98\%$ , Sigma Aldrich) were dissolved in 60 mL N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , VWR) under stirring to give a molar composition: ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}.$ /BDC/DMF $\fallingdotseq$ 1:1:500. The resulting mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave which an alumina ceramic was hung in. Afterwards the autoclave was placed in a convective oven (UF30, Memmert) and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days. After cooling, the membrane was washed with ethanol $99.85\%$ , VWR) and dried at 25 $^\circ\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum (Fistreem Vacuum Oven).
The Zr-MOFs adsorbent was collected from the mother solution in the above mentioned autoclave after membrane synthesis. The $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs powder was washed by ethanol with the assistance of centrifuge (Thermo Scientific Legend X1R) for later use.
## 2.2. Characterization methods
## 2.3. Batch adsorption experiments
Since the rate of fluoride adsorption was instructive for selecting the proper adsorption time, kinetics experiments were performed to determine the equilibrium time of adsorption. The adsorption kinetics experiments were carried out by adding $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs adsorbent into 250 mL polypropylene vessel containing $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of fluoride solution at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The solution $\mathrm{\pH}$ was adjusted to $7.0\pm0.1$ and then shaken at $150~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The above suspensions were withdrawn at a predetermined time and the supernatant solutions were pipetted and centrifuged for the determination of the remaining fluoride concentrations.
The adsorption isotherms experiments were performed in $15~\mathrm{\mL}$ polypropylene flasks containing $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of fluoride solution and $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbent. The concentrations of the fluoride solution ranged from 1 to $200~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , the $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted to $7.0\pm0.1$ . The flasks were shaken at $150\mathrm{rpm}$ in a shaker at three different temperatures, viz., 25, 35 and $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the supernatant solutions were pipetted and centrifuged for the determination of the remaining
fluoride concentrations.
To investigate the effect of $\mathsf{p H}$ on the fluoride removal, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ fluoride solutions containing $10\ \mathrm{\mg}$ of adsorbent were added into $15~\mathrm{mL}$ polypropylene flasks. The pH of the fluoride solution was adjusted from 3 to 12. To explore the influence of various co-existing anions such as chloride ion (Cl-), nitrate ion $(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\mathrm{^-})$ , sulfate ion $(\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-})$ , carbonate ion $(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-})$ , bicarbonate ion $\left(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\right)^{-}$ ) and phosphate ion $(\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-})$ on fluoride removal, 10 mL of $\boldsymbol{5}\mathrm{mg/L}$ fluoride solutions with $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbent were added into $15~\mathrm{mL}$ polypropylene flasks, the co-existing anions were set at four concentration levels (0.25, 0.5, 1.0 and $2.0~\mathrm{mM}$ . These flasks were then shaken at $150\ \mathrm{rpm}$ in a shaker at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the supernatant solutions were pipetted and centrifuged for the determination of the remaining fluoride concentrations.
All of the experiments were conducted in triplicate and the averages of the results were used for data analyses. The adsorption percentage and adsorption capacity $(q_{e},\mathrm{mg/g)}$ were calculated from the following equations [54]:
Adsorption percentage $(\%)=\ \frac{C_{0}-C_{e}}{C_{0}}{\times}100\%$ (1) $\ \mathrm{Adsorptioncapacity}q_{e}={\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})V}{m}}$ (2)
where $C_{0}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ ( $\mathrm{\mg/L})$ are the initial and final equilibrium concentrations of fluoride, $V\left(\mathrm{mL}\right)$ is the volume of solution, $m$ (mg) is the mass of the adsorbent.
## 2.4. Desorption and regeneration experiments
The regeneration of an adsorbent was the most important aspect for an economical technology. To investigate the regeneration of the adsorbents, fluoride-adsorbed $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs were immersed in sodium hydroxide solution $3\%$ wt) to be desorbed for fluoride adsorption again. Six consecutive
adsorption-desorption cycles were performed to explore the regeneration of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs adsorbent.
## 2.5. Membrane filter experiments
A filtration system was assembled to study the fluoride removal efficiency of Zr-MOFs membrane. The schematic of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane supported by alumina ceramic substrate (3.0 cm in diameter, $0.1\mathrm{cm}$ in thickness) and experimental setup for removal of fluoride were depicted in Fig. 1. The Zr-MOFs membrane on the back was removed as it does not make sense. A U-shaped tube $2.5~\mathrm{cm}$ in diameter was divided by $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs membrane into two compartments referred to as feed and permeate. With the help of a peristaltic pump, the water permeated through $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane while the fluoride ions were adsorbed. The temperature, flow rate and initial concentration of the fluoride solutions were changed as specified for each filter experiment, the filtered water amount was also tested.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Zr-MOFs adsorbent and membrane
Fig. 2a presented the XRD pattern of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs adsorbent and membrane comparing with the simulated Zr-MOFs structure from the CIF-file in Diamond, which was consistent with the other studies [55-58]. The pattern of the as-obtained $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs matched perfectly with the simulated pattern. The diffraction peaks and relative diffraction intensities at $7^{\circ}47^{\prime}$ , $8^{\circ}64^{\prime}$ , $25^{\circ}89^{\prime}$ were found to be in agreement with the (111), (002) and (244) facet of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs crystal, proving the formation of $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs. On the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs membrane, there was a strong peak belonged to ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ substrate compared to the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs [59]. To further study and quantify the pore structure of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs powders and membrane, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm and BJH adsorption pore size distribution of the obtained
The TGA technique was used to investigate the thermal behavior of Zr-MOFs powders, which could provide the necessary data for the following decomposition process. The TGA curves for the pyrolysis of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs were recorded in nitrogen atmosphere with a heating rate of $10~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ and shown in Fig. 3a. The mass loss of $4.582\%$ at 100°C was assigned to the evaporation of the adsorbed water while the mass loss of $10.274\%$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was attributed to the decrease of the DMF solvent [60-62]. At $200{-}450^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the TGA curve kept steady, indicating that the $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs was thermostable at this temperature range. At $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , there was a mass loss of $30.624\%$ , it was ascribed to the decomposition of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs to $\mathbf{{ZrO}}_{2}$ [63, 64]. From the results we can conclude that the as-synthesized Zr-MOFs were thermostable enough to be used in fluoride adsorption.
The fourier transform infrared spectroscopy spectrum of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs powders was shown in Fig. 3b, which was consistent with other studies [65-69]. The weak band detected at $1660~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was assigned to the stretching vibrations of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ in the carboxylic acid present in BDC [65]. Typical bands representing the O–C–O asymmetric stretching in the BDC ligand was observed at $1585~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [66]. The small band at $1506~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ represented the typical vibration present in a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ of a benzene ring, while the band at $1395~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was linked to the O–C–O symmetric stretching in the carboxylate group of the BDC ligands [67]. The peaks at 814, 746, and $673~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were due to OH and $\mathrm{C-H}$ vibration in the BDC ligands [57]. These results indicated the formation of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs using BDC ligands.
The SEM and TEM images of the Zr-MOFs adsorbents were presented in Fig. 4a and Fig. 4b. Zr-MOFs cubes with $120\mathrm{nm}$ in size were successfully obtained. The EDS pattern and elemental mappings of the cross section of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane were depicted in Fig. 5. Both signal of Zr-MOFs membrane $\mathrm{Zr}$ signal, C signal, O signal) and alumina substrate (Al signal, O signal) can be observed from the EDS pattern in Fig. 5a. After solvothermal synthesis, a continuous polycrystalline $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane with thickness of $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ was formed on the alumina substrate without any visible cracks or pinholes, as shown in Fig. 5b. This could be explained that the BDC ligands acted as a surface modifier of the substrate during the synthesis via constructing coordination bonds between the carboxylate oxgens and aluminum atoms from substrate [70] so that the nucleation and growth of Zr-MOFs on the alumina substrate were promoted to a large extent. A sharp transition between Zr-MOFs membrane (Zr signal, C signal) and alumina substrate (Al signal) was observed from the elemental mapping images, revealing that no detectable Zr-MOFs crystals nucleated into the bulk alumina substrate.
## 3.2. Results of batch experiments
## 3.2.1. Adsorption kinetics
The adsorption kinetics of fluoride on $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs at $5\mathrm{mg/L}$ fluoride solution with an adsorbent loading of $0.5\ \mathrm{g/L}$ at $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ were depicted in Fig. 6. The adsorption happened rapidly in the first 1 minute and reached equilibrium in 20 minutes. To further quantify the changes of fluoride adsorption with time on $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs adsorbent, the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models were used to simulate adsorption kinetics. The two kinetics models were generally
expressed in the following equation [15]:
$$
\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})=\ln q_{e}-\frac{K_{1}t}{2.303}
$$
$$
\frac{t}{{q_{t}}}=\frac{1}{{K_{2}}{q_{e}}^{2}}+\frac{t}{{q_{e}}}
$$
where ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and $\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the adsorption capacity $\mathrm{(mg/g)}$ of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs at time t and at equilibrium. $\mathbf{K}_{1}$ $(\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ and ${\bf K}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{\dot{\g}/m g}}$ min) are the equilibrium rate constant of pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order adsorption, respectively.
## 3.2.2. Adsorption isotherms
## 3.2.3. Adsorption thermodynamic parameters analysis
The thermodynamic parameters $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ , $\Delta\mathsf{S}^{\circ}$ , and $\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}$ ) for fluoride adsorption on $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs were calculated from the temperature-dependent adsorption isotherms. It is used to define whether the process is endothermic or exothermic, spontaneous or non-spontaneous. The standard free energy change $(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ})$ can be calculated from the following equation [73]:
$$
\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}=-\mathbf{R}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{ln}\mathbf{K}^{\circ}
$$
The standard enthalpy change $(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ})$ and the standard entropy $(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ})$ are calculated from the linear plot of ln $\mathbf{K}^{\circ}$ versus $1/\mathrm{T}$ for fluoride adsorption on the $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs in the following equation [74]:
$$
\ln{K^{0}}=\frac{{\Delta S}^{0}}{R}-\frac{{{\Delta H}^{0}}}{R T}
$$
## 3.2.4. Effects of pH and co-existing ions
## 3.3. Adsorption mechanism
To further investigate the material characteristics and design the application purpose of the new adsorbent, it was highly important to know the adsorption mechanism. To reveal the adsorption mechanism, Zr-MOFs before and after fluoride adsorption were characterized by FT-IR and XPS. The FT-IR spectra of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs before and after fluoride adsorption were depicted in Fig. 9a.
After fluoride adsorption, the bending vibrations of OH band at $673~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ decreased strongly in intensity while a new peak assigned to Zr-F stretching and F-Zr-F bending vibration appeared at $472\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , that could be due to the formation of fluoride complexes with Zr [75-78]. Furthermore, with the fluoride concentration increased, the increase and decrease of peaks at 472 and 673 cm seemed to be more apparent. The results demonstrated the important roles of surface hydroxyl groups and $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ active sites in fluoride adsorption process.
The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs before and after fluoride adsorption were depicted in Fig. 9b. The new observed binding energy around 833 and $685~\mathrm{eV}$ after fluoride removal were assigned to FAuger and F1s photoelectrons, indicating that fluoride was effectively adsorbed on $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs [2, 7, 79].  The $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ and O1s spectra were divided into some small peaks to further study the reactions of hydroxyl groups and $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ active sites in fluoride adsorption .
The Zr3d spectra of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs before and after adsorption were displayed in Fig. 9c. Before adsorption, prominent peaks from $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ were located at 184.83 and $182.47\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. However, after fluoride adsorption at $200~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , the $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks shifted to a higher binding energy by about $0.15\mathrm{eV}$ at 184.98 and $182.62\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. After fluoride adsorption at $\mathrm{1000mg/L}$ , the $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks shifted to a higher binding energy by 0.45 eV at 185.28 and $182.92\ \mathrm{eV}.$ This was because the formation of new zirconium species (possible candidates include $\mathrm{ZrF_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxyfluorides). Bonding to fluorine caused the loss of electron density at zirconium, which in turn raised the binding energies of $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks [75, 80]. Similar trends were reported for fluorinated zirconium oxide [81], plasma fluorinated zirconia [82] and zirconia glass after fluorination pretreatment [83].
The O1s spectra of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs before and after adsorption were displayed in Fig. 9d. Every spectrum was divided into four peaks at 529.80 (Zr-O), 530.95 (Zr-OH), 531.74 $\scriptstyle(=0)$ and 532.96 eV $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , respectively [4]. After fluoride adsorption with initial fluoride concentrations of 200 and $\mathrm{1000~mg/L}$ , the area radio of $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -OH peak decreased from $46.44\%$ to $42.77\%$ and $39.06\%$ respectively, which indicated that the hydroxyl groups were involved in fluoride adsorption. Combined with the results of FT-IR analyses, it could be concluded that the adsorption mechanism was hydroxyl groups bonded to Zr species exchanging with fluoride.
## 3.4. Desorption and regeneration
## 3.5. Practical implications
In order to investigate the practical implications of our research, the $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs was used for the treatment of the underground water with a high fluoride content in Xingwang Village, Inner Mongolia of China, which has caused severe adverse health effects on local people. The characteristics of the underground water samples were displayed in Table 3, demonstrating the complexity of substances and high concentration of fluoride $(2.82\mathrm{mg/L})$ in the water sample. The effect of adsorbent dose on fluoride removal of the water sample after 180-min treatment was shown in Fig. 10. The residual fluoride concentration decreased with increasing adsorbent dose. At an adsorbent dose of $0.05~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the fluoride concentration in the water sample was reduced from $2.82~\mathrm{mg/L}$ to about $1.27~\mathrm{mg/L}$ after the treatment, which completely meets the guideline limit of fluoride in drinking water regulated by the WHO ( $\mathrm{1.5~mg/L})$ . This application implied that the adsorbents were able to be used in practical treatment of drinking water.
## 3.6. Membrane experiments
## 3.6.1. Effect of initial concentration of fluoride
The effect of initial concentration of fluoride on fluoride removal was investigated and depicted in Fig. 11a. With flow rates of 15 mL/min, fluoride solutions with different initial concentrations
were used in filtration experiments through $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane with thickness of $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . As initial concentration of fluoride increased from 1 to $10~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , the fluoride removal decreased from $97.14\%$ to $85.81\%$ , maintaining high fluoride removal efficiency.
## 3.6.2. Effect of flow rate
The effect of flow rate on fluoride removal was studied and displayed in Fig. 11b. Filtration experiments with $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs membrane $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in thickness were performed with flow rates of 4 - 40 mL/min whereas the initial concentration of fluoride was 5 mg/L. The fluoride removal decreased from $96.21\%$ to $80.31\%$ as flow rate increased from 4 to 40 mL/min. This was because more contact time was obtained when the flow rate was low.
## 3.6.3. Effect of temperature
The effect of temperature on fluoride removal was explored and depicted in Fig. 11c. At temperature range of $10-25~^{\circ}C_{\circ}^{\circ},$ fluoride removal kept steady. At temperature higher than $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the fluoride removal increased slightly with temperature.
## 3.6.4. Amount of filtered water
The maximum amount of water that $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs membrane can deal with was explored and depicted in Fig. 11d. The $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane was invalid in fluoride removal as the value of $\mathrm{{\calC}_{\mathrm{e}}/{\cal C}_{0}}$ was increased to 1. When the fluoride concentration were 5, 8 and $10\mathrm{mg/L}$ , with membrane thickness of $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and flow rate of $15\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ , the maximum amount of water that $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane (with area of $4.91~\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) can deal with were 2700, 2540 and $2290~\mathrm{mL}$ . Thus, the fluoride removal abilities of the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane were 5510, 5173, and $4664~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{m}^{2}$ when the fluoride concentrations were 5, 8 and $10~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , respectively.
## 3.6.5. Recyclability of Zr-MOFs membrane
## 4. Conclusions
Both $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs adsorbent and $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane were prepared and used for fluoride removal for the first time. For adsorption technique, the obtained $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs adsorbent with large specific surface area of $740.28~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ presented much higher adsorption capacities than most of the other fluoride adsorbents. The removal of fluoride was fitted well with Langmuir model while the maximuim adsorption capacity was $102.40~\mathrm{mg/g}$ when the fluoride concentration was $200~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . The adsorption reached equilibrium in 20 minutes, and fitted into a pseudo-second-order kinetic model. The fluoride removal mechanism demonstrated that both surface hydroxyl groups and Zr(IV) active sites played important roles in fluoride adsorption process. For membrane technique, the $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs membrane was prepared through a simple process of suction filtration. The highest percentage removal of fluoride by $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs membrane was $98\%$ . The fluoride removal abilities of Zr-MOFs membrane with $20~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness could reach 5510, 5173, and $4664~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{m}^{2}$ when fluoride concentrations were 5, 8 and $10~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , respectively. This study indicated that $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOFs membrane could be developed into a very viable technology for highly effective removal of fluoride from
drinking water.
